//bruteforce
There are n × m possible pairs.
Operation	Complexity
Generate all pairs	O(nm)
Insert all pairs into heap	O(nm log(nm))
Extract k pairs	O(k log(nm))

Overall Time Complexity:

O(nm log(nm) + k log(nm))

Or more compactly:

O((nm + k) log(nm))

Space Complexity:

O(nm)

All nm pairs are stored in the priority queue.

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> kSmallestPairs(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2, int k) {
        priority_queue<pair<int,pair<int,int>>,vector<pair<int,pair<int,int>>>,greater<pair<int,pair<int,int>>>>pq;

        for(int i = 0; i < nums1.size(); i++)
        {
            for(int j = 0; j < nums2.size(); j++)
            {
                int sum = nums1[i] + nums2[j];
                pq.push({sum, {nums1[i], nums2[j]}});
            }
        }

        vector<vector<int>>ans;
        while(k--)
        {
            auto [wt,p] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();

            auto [u,v] = p;

            vector<int>li;
            li.push_back(u);
            li.push_back(v);
            ans.push_back(li);
        }

        return ans;

        

    }
};


//optimal
**Idea:**

Since both arrays are already sorted, we don't need to generate all possible pairs.

For every element in `nums1`, initially insert a pair with `nums2[0]`.

When we extract a pair `(i, j)`, the next possible candidate from the same row is `(i, j+1)`.

This way, we explore only the necessary pairs.

**Time Complexity:**

Initially, we insert at most min(n, k) pairs.

Then we perform at most k extractions and at most k insertions.

| Operation              | Complexity                  |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Initial heap insertion | O(min(n, k) log(min(n, k))) |
| Extract k elements     | O(k log(min(n, k)))         |
| Insert next candidates | O(k log(min(n, k)))         |

**Overall Time Complexity:**

O((min(n, k) + k) log(min(n, k)))

More simply:

O(k log(min(n, k)))

**Space Complexity:**

The heap stores at most min(n, k) elements, and the answer stores k pairs.

- Auxiliary heap space: O(min(n, k))
- Output space: O(k)

**Total Space Complexity:**

O(k + min(n, k))
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> kSmallestPairs(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2, int k) {
        priority_queue<pair<int,pair<int,int>>,vector<pair<int,pair<int,int>>>,greater<pair<int,pair<int,int>>>>pq;

            int n = nums1.size();
            int m = nums2.size();
            for(int i = 0; i < min(n,k); i++)
            {
                pq.push({nums1[i] + nums2[0], {i,0}});
            }
        

        vector<vector<int>>ans;
        while(k--)
        {
            auto [wt,p] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();

            auto [u,v] = p;

            ans.push_back({nums1[u],nums2[v]});

            if(v+1<nums2.size())
            {
                pq.push({nums1[u] + nums2[v + 1],{u, v + 1}});
            }
        }

        return ans;

        

    }
};
